A Forward Proxy (usually just called a "proxy") is an intermediary server that sits in front of a group of client devices (like your laptop, phone, or corporate workstations). When you try to access a website on the internet, your request doesn't go straight to the website. Instead, it goes to the forward proxy first. The proxy then turns around, requests the website on your behalf, gets the response, and hands it back to you.

In short: A forward proxy acts on behalf of the client to evaluate, filter, and mask outbound traffic before it reaches the wider internet.

If you plug your laptop into an office or university Wi-Fi network, the organization almost always routes your traffic through a forward proxy.

The Scenario: You try to navigate to a gambling website or a known malware-hosting domain during your break.

How the Proxy Acts: Your browser sends the request to the corporate forward proxy. The proxy parses the destination domain, checks it against the company's "Blocklist," drops your request, and returns a "Website Blocked by Administrator" page instead. It completely insulates the organization's network from malicious external content.

Commercial VPNs and web proxies act as forward proxies to shield consumer identities.

The Scenario: You are sitting in India, but you want to test how a streaming website behaves for a user located in London.

How the Proxy Acts: You connect to a forward proxy server physically located in the UK. When you type in the streaming website's URL, the forward proxy makes the request. To the streaming platform's load balancers, the request looks like it originated from a local UK resident. The proxy receives the video stream and forwards the packets back down to your machine in India.
